The induced demand effect means that adding road capacity increases traffic; the inverse also holds — reducing car convenience through transit investment reduces car trips.
Los Angeles' new Metro system, combined with bike infrastructure, removed enough cars to reduce downtown congestion by 15% and cut transport CO₂ by 12% between 2010 and 2019.
Converting a single lane of traffic to a dedicated bus lane can move 3× as many people per hour — public transit is simply more space-efficient than private cars.
